.input|xwiki/2.1
.# Code macros that can't be written using the Markdown code syntax (code spans can't have a language, and code
.# blocks can't have parameters other than the language) are written using the macro syntax, which keeps their content
.# exactly (including the white spaces). When the macro syntax can't be used either (e.g. when the content contains the
.# closing tag, or when the inline macro is the first content of a list item or definition description where it would be
.# read back as a standalone macro), the Markdown code syntax is used. An inline macro starting a paragraph is preceded
.# by a space so that it's not read back as a standalone macro. See code24.test for the parsing of the generated
.# Markdown.
.#-----------------------------------------------------
